ut << \\"\\\\n\\";\\n}"}	2026-07-03 07:10:28.499	2026-07-03 07:10:28.499
70dcff98-077e-4484-9918-2e227e238baa	Tribonacci Number	The Tribonacci sequence Tn is defined as follows:\n- T0 = 0\n- T1 = 1\n- T2 = 1\n- Tn = Tn-1 + Tn-2 + Tn-3 for n >= 3\n\nGiven n, return the value of Tn modulo 1,000,000,007 (10^9 + 7).\n\n**Input Format:**\n- A single line containing an integer n.\n\n**Output Format:**\n- A single integer: Tn % 1000000007.	EASY	{dynamic-programming,tabulation}	ccf6772f-27d4-4001-bd5e-763e3c380ba0	[{"input": "4", "title": "Example 1", "output": "4", "explanation": "T0=0, T1=1, T2=1, T3=0+1+1=2, T4=1+1+2=4."}, {"input": "25", "title": "Example 2", "output": "1389537"}]	0 <= n <= 10^5	This is very similar to Fibonacci, but instead of keeping track of the last two values, you must keep track of the last three values.	**Approach: Dynamic Programming (Iterative)**\n\nUse three variables `a`, `b`, and `c` initialized to T0, T1, and T2 respectively.\nIterate from 3 to n, updating:\nnext = (a + b + c) % MOD\na = b\nb = c\nc = next\n\n**Time Complexity:** O(n)\n**Space Complexity:** O(1)	[{"input": "4", "output": "4"}, {"input": "25", "output": "1389537"}, {"input": "0", "output": "0"}, {"input": "1", "output": "1"}, {"input": "2", "output": "1"}, {"input": "3", "output": "2"}, {"input": "10", "output": "149"}, {"input": "100", "output": "92295268"}, {"input": "1000", "output": "887456284"}, {"input": "100000", "output": "30627177"}, {"input": "13", "output": "927"}, {"input": "50", "output": "568701031"}, {"input": "-1", "output": "1"}, {"input": "30", "output": "29249425"}, {"input": "5", "output": "7"}, {"input": "101", "output": "347873931"}, {"input": "22", "output": "223317"}, {"input": "999", "output": "78728823"}, {"input": "15", "output": "3136"}, {"input": "8", "output": "44"}, {"input": "-3", "output": "1"}, {"input": "12", "output": "504"}, {"input": "500", "output": "497054932"}, {"input": "24", "output": "755476"}, {"input": "7", "output": "24"}, {"input": "95", "output": "968310390"}, {"input": "110", "output": "26991134"}, {"input": "17", "output": "10609"}, {"input": "105", "output": "712685028"}, {"input": "32", "output": "98950096"}]	{"cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\\nusing namespace std;\\n\\nint main() {\\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\\n    \\n    // Read input and solve\\n    \\n    return 0;\\n}"}	{"cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main() {\\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\\n    int n;\\n    if (cin >> n) {\\n        if (n == 0) {\\n            cout << 0 << \\"\\n\\";\\n            return 0;\\n        }\\n        if (n == 1 || n == 2) {\\n            cout << 1 << \\"\\n\\";\\n            return 0;\\n        }\\n        long long a = 0, b = 1, c = 1;\\n        long long MOD = 1000000007;\\n        for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {\\n            long long d = (a + b + c) % MOD;\\n            a = b;\\n            b = c;\\n            c = d;\\n        }\\n        cout << c << \\"\\n\\";\\n    }\\n    return 0;\\n}"}	2026-07-03 07:10:28.502	2026-07-03 07:10:28.502
f838fd64-341b-47f7-8c71-985207a4beba	Path Sum	Given the root of a binary tree and an integer targetSum, return true if the tree has a root-to-leaf path such that adding up all the values along the path equals targetSum.\n\nA leaf is a node with no children.\n\n**Input Format:**\n- First line: BFS level-order representation of the binary tree (using 'null' for empty nodes).\n- Second line: The integer targetSum.\n\n**Output Format:**\n- 'true' or 'false'.	EASY	{tree,binary-tree,dfs}	ccf6772f-27d4-4001-bd5e-763e3c380ba0	[{"input": "5 4 8 11 null 13 4 7 2 null null null 1\\n22", "title": "Example 1", "output": "true", "explanation": "The root-to-leaf path 5 -> 4 -> 11 -> 2 sums to 22."}, {"input": "1 2 3\\n5", "title": "Example 2", "output": "false", "explanation": "The root-to-leaf paths are 1 -> 2 (sum 3) and 1 -> 3 (sum 4). None of them sum to 5."}]	The number of nodes in the tree is in the range [0, 5000].\n-1000 <= Node.val <= 1000\n-10^5 <= targetSum <= 10^5	Traverse the tree recursively. At each node, subtract the node value from the targetSum and pass it to the children. A path is valid if you reach a leaf node with a value equal to the remaining sum.	**Approach: Recursive DFS**\nFor each node, if it is a leaf, check if its value equals the remaining sum. Otherwise, recursively search the left and right subtrees with targetSum minus the current node's value.\nTime Complexity: O(N)\nSpace Complexity: O(H) where H is the height of the tree.	[{"input": "5 4 8 11 null 13 4 7 2 null null null 1\\n22", "output": "true"}, {"input": "1 2 3\\n5", "output": "false"}, {"input": "1 2\\n0", "output": "false"}, {"input": "1 2\\n3", "output": "true"}, {"input": "null\\n0", "output": "false"}, {"input": "1\\n1", "output": "true"}, {"input": "1 -2 -3\\n-1", "output": "true"}, {"input": "1 2 3 4 5\\n7", "output": "true"}, {"input": "1 2 3 4 5\\n8", "output": "true"}, {"input": "1 2 3 4 5\\n5", "output": "false"}, {"input": "5 5\\n5 4 3 2 1", "output": "false"}, {"input": "5 4 8 